miconductor L36:   * cite21†Eco-friendly L37:   * cite22†ESG L38: ###\nL39: \nL40: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL41: \nL42: [Select]\nL43: \nL44: [Input] cite0†search L45: \nL46: [Button: 갤러형으로 보기] [Button: 목록형으로 보기]\nL47: 【0†SKC Invests KRW 281 Billion in Absolics, Accelerating Commercialization of Glass Substrates ○ SKC announces participation in Absolics' shareholder allocation rights issue...\nL48: Acquiring over 190,000 new shares based on its stake○​ Accelerating the two-track strategy for glass substrates...Commencing reliability evaluations for embedding products and preparing for non-embedding PoC within the yearSKC is accelerating the commercialization of glass substrates to secure a leading position in the next-generation packaging market by participating in the rights issue of Absolics, its semiconductor glass substrate business investment arm.SKC announced on the 4th that its board of directors resolved to make a cash investment of approximately KRW 281 billion by participating in a shareholder allocation rights issue initiated by Absolics.\nL49: SKC plans to acquire 192,416 new shares, allocated in proportion to its current stake (70.05%).This participation is a follow-up measure to SKC’s KRW 1.1671 trillion capital increase carried out in the first half of this year. SKC previously outlined a blueprint to invest approximately KRW 590 billion of the secured funds into the glass substrate business over the next three years.\nL50: This current investment marks the first execution of funds based on that decision, and SKC plans to inject the remaining capital in stages, aligned with the business's progress.Absolics is currently expediting its preparations for the commercialization of glass substrates, with a strategic focus on both embedding and non-embedding products.\n"
